const LESSONS=[
{title:'The big picture',tag:'Control essentials',page:'1',minutes:2,lead:'Controlling closes the gap between the plan and what actually happens.',body:`<p><strong>Controlling means assessing and regulating performance</strong> against adopted plans, issued instructions, and established principles. It tells managers how well people and processes are working so they can support better performance.</p><h3>Same purpose, different timing</h3><div class="concept-grid"><div class="concept"><span class="eyebrow">Before</span><h4>Feedforward</h4><p>Monitor inputs and anticipate problems before they affect the system.</p></div><div class="concept"><span class="eyebrow">During</span><h4>Concurrent</h4><p>Monitor activities as they happen and check that they meet standards.</p></div><div class="concept"><span class="eyebrow">After</span><h4>Feedback</h4><p>Evaluate completed activities and use the results to improve future work.</p></div></div><div class="callout peach"><strong>Memory hook · Before, during, after.</strong><p>Feedforward looks ahead. Concurrent stays alongside. Feedback looks back.</p></div><h3>Imagine one ward shift</h3><ul><li><strong>Before:</strong> check whether supplies are ready for the shift → feedforward.</li><li><strong>During:</strong> observe practice while care is happening → concurrent.</li><li><strong>After:</strong> review last month's completed reports to improve the next month → feedback.</li></ul><p class="small muted">These ward examples were created to help apply the handout's definitions.</p>`,check:'A manager reviews last month’s incident reports to improve next month’s practice. Which control type?',answer:'Feedback control: the activity is already complete, and its results inform future work.'},
{title:'Evaluate with purpose',tag:'Evaluation',page:'1',minutes:3,lead:'A useful evaluation makes improvement specific, visible, and fair.',body:`<h3>Why conduct evaluation?</h3><ul><li>Ensure quality nursing care and set sensible objectives that staff can meet.</li><li>Provide standards for comparison and help employees monitor their own performance.</li><li>Highlight quality problems and areas needing priority attention.</li><li>Show the costs of poor quality and justify the use of resources.</li><li>Provide feedback that supports improvement.</li></ul><h3>Principles to remember</h3><ul><li>Base evaluation on <strong>behavioral performance standards</strong> and allow enough time to observe behavior.</li><li>Give employees their job description, performance standards, and evaluation form.</li><li>Include both <strong>satisfactory and unsatisfactory</strong> results.</li><li>Prioritize areas needing improvement.</li><li>Schedule the conference at a convenient time for both people.</li><li>Structure the evaluation report and conference.</li></ul><h3>Four qualities of an evaluation tool</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Quality</th><th>What the handout emphasizes</th><th>Recall cue</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Objectivity</td><td>Free from bias.</td><td>Fair judgment.</td></tr><tr><td>Reliability</td><td>Accurate. Think dependable, consistent measurement.</td><td>Dependable result.</td></tr><tr><td>Validity</td><td>Relevant to what is being evaluated.</td><td>Measures the right thing.</td></tr><tr><td>Sensitivity</td><td>Detects fine differences among the criteria measured.</td><td>Notices small differences.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout"><strong>Memory hook · O–R–V–S</strong><p>Objective, Reliable, Valid, Sensitive: fair, dependable, relevant, detailed. The recall cues expand the handout's short labels.</p></div>`,check:'A tool distinguishes small differences between two nurses’ performance. Which characteristic is this?',answer:'Sensitivity. Objectivity concerns bias; validity concerns relevance; reliability concerns dependable measurement.'},
{title:'Close the control loop',tag:'Cycle & standards',page:'1',minutes:3,lead:'Set the target, measure reality, compare, and act.',body:`<ol class="step-list"><li><div><strong>Establish standards, objectives, and measurement methods.</strong><br>Decide what good performance looks like and how to assess it. The handout lists task analysis and quality control as methods for measuring nursing care.</div></li><li><div><strong>Measure actual performance.</strong><br>Use an ongoing, repetitive process. Frequency depends on the activity. Measurement may be scheduled or occur at periodic, unannounced intervals.</div></li><li><div><strong>Compare results with standards and objectives.</strong><br>Identify whether actual performance matches expectations.</div></li><li><div><strong>Reinforce strengths and take corrective action.</strong><br>Keep effective performance going and address gaps to improve results.</div></li></ol><h3>What exactly are we measuring?</h3><div class="concept-grid"><div class="concept"><span class="eyebrow">The setup</span><h4>Structure</h4><p>The agency's structure or management system for delivering care: staffing, equipment, resources.</p></div><div class="concept"><span class="eyebrow">The actions</span><h4>Process</h4><p>What the nurse does in relation to the nursing process: how care is performed.</p></div><div class="concept"><span class="eyebrow">The results</span><h4>Outcome</h4><p>What results from the care provided: changes in patient outcomes.</p></div></div><div class="callout peach"><strong>Memory hook · Setup → actions → results.</strong><p>Enough IV pumps? Structure. Was a procedure carried out as expected? Process. Did fall rates decrease? Outcome.</p></div>`,check:'The manager identifies that performance is below the established standard. What should follow the comparison?',answer:'Reinforce strengths and take corrective action to improve performance. Comparison alone does not complete the control process.'},
{title:'Appraisal that helps',tag:'Appraisal essentials',page:'1',minutes:3,lead:'Performance appraisal compares an employee’s work with standards over a specified period.',body:`<h3>What is appraisal for?</h3><p>It reflects how well nursing personnel have performed during a defined time. The handout lists these purposes:</p><ul><li>Determine salary standards; select qualified employees for promotion; identify unsatisfactory performance for possible demotion.</li><li>Make an inventory of talent and determine training needs.</li><li>Improve work-group performance and communication.</li><li>Establish supervisory performance standards.</li><li>Discover employees' aspirations, recognize accomplishments, and explain where they stand.</li></ul><h3>Make the standard visible</h3><ul><li>Base appraisal on a standard.</li><li>Let the employee contribute to developing the standard.</li><li>Ensure the employee knows the standard <strong>in advance</strong> and knows the sources of appraisal data.</li><li>Use an appraiser who has <strong>observed the work</strong> and whom the employee trusts and respects.</li></ul><h3>Informal vs. formal appraisal</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Informal</th><th>Formal</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Incidental observation during nursing care.</td><td>Regular, methodical collection of objective facts.</td></tr><tr><td>A manager notices that a nurse missed hand hygiene during care.</td><td>A scheduled chart audit checks documentation accuracy and completeness against expectations.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout"><strong>Ask: incidental observation or organized review?</strong><p>Both can identify performance issues. The difference is how the appraisal is conducted.</p></div>`,check:'Who is best placed to appraise a nurse: someone with a senior title but no observation, or a trusted appraiser who has observed the work?',answer:'The trusted appraiser who has observed the employee’s work. The handout emphasizes both direct observation and trust/respect.'},
{title:'Tools, without the bias',tag:'Appraisal tools & pitfalls',page:'2',minutes:4,lead:'Recognize the tool being used—and the judgment trap that can distort it.',body:`<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Appraisal tool</th><th>How to recognize it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Essay</td><td>A free-form narrative of strengths and areas needing improvement.</td></tr><tr><td>Ranking</td><td>Orders employees relative to coworkers, with respect to qualifications.</td></tr><tr><td>Checklists</td><td>A compilation of expected nursing performances or behavior descriptions.</td></tr><tr><td>Rating scale</td><td>Rates tasks or desired behaviors, including their presence, absence, or extent.</td></tr><tr><td>Forced-choice comparison</td><td>Selects statements that best or least describe the nurse from groups of favorable and unfavorable statements.</td></tr><tr><td>Anecdotal recording</td><td>A descriptive account of the nurse's experience with a person or group; can validate technical skills and interpersonal relationships.</td></tr><tr><td>Peer review</td><td>Colleagues of the same profession, rank, and setting evaluate performance against accepted standards.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Three checklist forms</h3><ul><li><strong>Weighted scale:</strong> behavioral statements representing desirable job behaviors.</li><li><strong>Forced checklist:</strong> select a desirable and an undesirable behavior for each employee.</li><li><strong>Simple checklist:</strong> check words or phrases that describe the employee's traits or behaviors.</li></ul><h3>Spot the appraisal pitfalls</h3><div class="concept-grid"><div class="concept"><h4>Halo effect</h4><p>One or two positive behaviors influence the assessment of everything else.</p></div><div class="concept"><h4>Horns effect</h4><p>Negative aspects overshadow other areas, making the overall assessment inaccurate.</p></div><div class="concept"><h4>Central tendency</h4><p>The appraiser avoids a true assessment and rates everyone as average.</p></div></div><div class="callout peach"><strong>Memory hook · Halo glows, horns darken, center flattens.</strong><p>A standout positive or negative should not replace assessment of each performance area. Everyone-average ratings hide real differences.</p></div>`,check:'A nurse is excellent at communication, so the appraiser gives top scores for every skill without checking the other areas. Which pitfall?',answer:'Halo effect: a positive aspect spills over into unrelated performance ratings.'},
{title:'Quality, checked',tag:'Quality assurance & audits',page:'2–3',minutes:4,lead:'Quality assurance evaluates care; improvement uses what we learn to make it better.',body:`<h3>Four ideas that fit together</h3><ul><li><strong>Quality assurance:</strong> evaluation applied to the healthcare system and the services delivered by health workers.</li><li><strong>Quality improvement program:</strong> continuous measurement and evaluation of structure, process, and outcome. Example: monitor hand hygiene and implement strategies to reduce infections.</li><li><strong>Benchmarking:</strong> compare products or services with the best-performing organizations. Example: compare patient satisfaction with top institutions.</li><li><strong>Quality of care:</strong> the degree to which services increase the likelihood of desired health outcomes for individuals and populations.</li></ul><p><strong>Nursing audit</strong> documents the quality of nursing care in relation to the nursing department's established standards.</p><h3>Audits have two separate lenses</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Lens</th><th>Type</th><th>Clue</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td rowspan="2">When?</td><td>Concurrent patient care audit</td><td>Care or charts are reviewed while the patient is still admitted.</td></tr><tr><td>Retrospective patient care audit</td><td>Review after discharge: charts, questionnaires, or interviews.</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="3">What?</td><td>Structure audit</td><td>The setting: finances, staffing structure, records systems, equipment, environment.</td></tr><tr><td>Process audit</td><td>How care was carried out; deviations from accepted best-practice process standards.</td></tr><tr><td>Outcome audit</td><td>Results of nursing interventions: fall rates, infection rates, recovery outcomes.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout peach"><strong>Don't mix up timing with focus.</strong><p>A review may be <em>concurrent</em> because the patient is admitted and focus on <em>process</em> because it checks how care is delivered. Read what the question asks.</p></div><p>The handout describes outcome audits as the most valid indicators of quality care. Keep that phrasing associated with the handout.</p><h3>People improve quality, too</h3><ul><li><strong>Peer review:</strong> collegial performance evaluation to promote practice excellence.</li><li><strong>Quality circles:</strong> workers doing similar work voluntarily meet regularly during normal working time under their supervisor's leadership.</li></ul>`,check:'A team checks whether care followed the expected procedure while the patient is still admitted. Classify it by timing, then by focus.',answer:'Timing: concurrent patient care audit. Focus: process audit. These classifications answer different questions.'},
{title:'Discipline with direction',tag:'Discipline foundations',page:'3',minutes:3,lead:'Discipline is a constructive way to build responsibility for performance and behavior.',body:`<h3>What strengthens self-discipline?</h3><ul><li>Commitment to the institution's vision, philosophy, goals, and objectives.</li><li>Professional laws and codes of conduct.</li><li>Understanding agency rules and regulations.</li><li>An atmosphere of mutual trust and confidence.</li><li>Peer and organizational pressure.</li></ul><h3>Build a sound disciplinary program</h3><p>Tailor the program to the institution's objectives. Include <strong>policies and procedures, uniform application of rules, a disciplinary committee, and orientation for new employees.</strong></p><h3>Six principles of successful implementation</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Principle</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Promptness</td><td>Address the violation promptly, rather than weeks later.</td></tr><tr><td>Fairness</td><td>Match the response to the severity of the offense.</td></tr><tr><td>Impartiality</td><td>Apply rules consistently without favoritism.</td></tr><tr><td>Non-punitiveness</td><td>Aim for correction and improvement rather than punishment.</td></tr><tr><td>Advance warning</td><td>Tell staff the rules and possible consequences beforehand.</td></tr><tr><td>Follow-through</td><td>Enforce the rules consistently; do not ignore recurring violations.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout"><strong>Memory hook · Now, fair, equal, improve, inform, follow.</strong><p>Promptness, fairness, impartiality, non-punitiveness, advance warning, follow-through.</p></div>`,check:'An experienced nurse and a new nurse are treated the same under the same rule. Is the strongest clue fairness or impartiality?',answer:'Impartiality: equal application without favoritism. Fairness focuses on matching the consequence to the severity of the offense.'},
{title:'The discipline ladder',tag:'Disciplinary actions',page:'3',minutes:3,lead:'Learn the progressive sequence described in the handout, including what each step contains.',body:`<ol class="step-list"><li><div><strong>Counseling and oral warning</strong><br>Discuss expectations for improvement, ways to correct the problem, and the possibility of further action if it happens again.</div></li><li><div><strong>Written warning</strong><br>State the problem and violated rule, consequences of continued behavior, the employee's commitment to corrective action, and planned follow-up.</div></li><li><div><strong>Suspension</strong><br>For a minor violation, the handout places suspension after evidence of oral and written warnings. It describes suspension rather than dismissal when management believes the employee can still be rehabilitated.</div></li><li><div><strong>Dismissal</strong><br>The handout places this after other disciplinary efforts fail. The committee must ensure the cause conforms to major-violation criteria in the policy manual.</div></li></ol><div class="callout peach"><strong>Memory hook · Talk → write → suspend → dismiss.</strong><p>This is the sequence taught in your handout. The policy manual and the committee's criteria remain part of the handout's dismissal requirements.</p></div><h3>Read the details in a case</h3><ul><li>Advice, improvement expectations, and a warning about repetition → counseling/oral warning.</li><li>Documented problem, violated rule, consequences, commitment, and follow-up → written warning.</li><li>Earlier warnings documented and improvement still considered possible → suspension in the handout's progression.</li><li>Other efforts failed and policy criteria confirmed → dismissal in the handout's progression.</li></ul>`,check:'What five elements belong in a written warning?',answer:'The problem; the violated rule; consequences of continued behavior; the employee’s commitment to corrective action; and follow-up action.'}
];
const FLASHCARDS=[
 [0,'What does controlling assess and regulate?','Performance against adopted plans, issued instructions, and established principles.'],
 [0,'Which control looks ahead at inputs?','Feedforward control: anticipate and prevent problems before they affect the system.'],
 [0,'Which control operates while work is happening?','Concurrent control: monitor ongoing activities in real time.'],
 [0,'Which control uses completed results for future improvement?','Feedback control. The activity is complete; its results guide future work.'],
 [1,'Name three reasons to evaluate nursing performance.','Examples: ensure quality care, identify priority problems, justify resources, set objectives, support self-monitoring, and give improvement feedback.'],
 [1,'What should evaluation be based on?','Behavioral performance standards, with enough observation time and both satisfactory and unsatisfactory results.'],
 [1,'What three documents should employees receive?','Their job description, performance standards, and evaluation form.'],
 [1,'Objectivity means…','The evaluation tool is free from bias.'],
 [1,'Reliability vs. validity: what is the difference?','The handout labels reliability as accuracy (think dependable measurement) and validity as relevance (measuring the right thing).'],
 [1,'What does sensitivity detect?','Fine differences among the criteria being measured.'],
 [2,'What are the four steps in the control process?','Establish standards/objectives/methods → measure actual performance → compare with standards/objectives → reinforce strengths and take corrective action.'],
 [2,'Structure, process, outcome: give the simplest cues.','Structure = setup or resources. Process = actions or how care is done. Outcome = results of care.'],
 [2,'What are the handout’s two methods for measuring nursing care?','Task analysis and quality control.'],
 [2,'How often is actual performance measured?','It is ongoing and repetitive; frequency depends on the activity. Measurement can be scheduled or periodic and unannounced.'],
 [3,'What is performance appraisal?','Evaluation of an employee’s performance against standards during a specified period.'],
 [3,'Name some purposes of performance appraisal.','Salary/promotion decisions, identifying unsatisfactory performance, talent inventory, training, group performance, communication, supervisory standards, aspirations, recognition, and telling staff where they stand.'],
 [3,'What makes an effective appraiser?','They have observed the employee’s work and are trusted and respected by the employee.'],
 [3,'What must employees know before appraisal?','The standard in advance and the sources of appraisal data. They should also have input into developing the standard.'],
 [3,'Informal vs. formal appraisal?','Informal = incidental observation during work. Formal = regular, methodical collection of objective facts against expectations.'],
 [4,'Which tool is also called a free-form review?','Essay: a narrative description of strengths and areas needing improvement.'],
 [4,'Ranking vs. rating scale?','Ranking compares employees with coworkers. A rating scale scores tasks or behaviors and their extent.'],
 [4,'Name the three checklist forms.','Weighted scale, forced checklist, and simple checklist.'],
 [4,'Forced checklist vs. forced-choice comparison?','Forced checklist selects a desirable and an undesirable behavior. Forced-choice comparison chooses the statements that best/least describe the nurse from grouped choices.'],
 [4,'What is anecdotal recording?','A descriptive account of a nurse’s experience with a person or group, including evidence of technical skills or interpersonal relationships.'],
 [4,'Who performs peer review?','Colleagues of the same profession, rank, and setting evaluate performance against accepted standards.'],
 [4,'Halo, horns, central tendency: distinguish them.','Halo = positives spill over. Horns = negatives spill over. Central tendency = everyone is rated average.'],
 [5,'What is quality assurance?','Evaluation applied to the healthcare system and health services provided by health workers.'],
 [5,'Quality improvement vs. benchmarking?','Quality improvement continuously measures and evaluates structure, process, and outcome. Benchmarking compares with best-performing organizations.'],
 [5,'What does quality of care mean?','The degree to which services increase the likelihood of desired health outcomes for individuals and populations.'],
 [5,'What is a nursing audit?','Documentation of nursing care quality in relation to the nursing department’s established standards.'],
 [5,'Concurrent vs. retrospective patient care audit?','Concurrent = while the patient is admitted. Retrospective = after discharge, using charts, questionnaires, or interviews.'],
 [5,'What does a structure audit examine?','The setting and resources for care: finances, staffing structure, records systems, equipment, and environment.'],
 [5,'What does a process audit examine?','How nursing care was carried out, compared with accepted best-practice process standards.'],
 [5,'What does an outcome audit examine?','Results of nursing interventions, such as fall or infection rates. The handout calls these the most valid indicators of quality care.'],
 [5,'What is a quality circle?','Workers doing similar work who voluntarily meet regularly during normal working time under their supervisor’s leadership.'],
 [6,'What is the purpose of discipline?','Constructive responsibility for one’s own performance and behavior.'],
 [6,'Name factors that influence self-discipline.','Institutional commitment, professional laws/codes, understanding rules, mutual trust/confidence, and peer/organizational pressure.'],
 [6,'What four elements support a disciplinary program?','Policies/procedures, uniform application of rules, a disciplinary committee, and orientation for new staff.'],
 [6,'Fairness vs. impartiality?','Fairness = response matches severity. Impartiality = rules applied without favoritism.'],
 [6,'Promptness vs. follow-through?','Promptness = address the issue promptly. Follow-through = consistently enforce rules and address recurring violations.'],
 [6,'What are non-punitiveness and advance warning?','Non-punitiveness aims for correction rather than punishment. Advance warning tells staff rules and consequences beforehand.'],
 [7,'What is the handout’s disciplinary sequence?','Counseling/oral warning → written warning → suspension → dismissal.'],
 [7,'What belongs in counseling and oral warning?','Expected improvement, ways to correct the problem, and warning that repetition may lead to further disciplinary action.'],
 [7,'What belongs in a written warning?','Problem, violated rule, consequences of continued behavior, employee commitment to corrective action, and follow-up.'],
 [7,'When does the handout describe suspension rather than dismissal?','After evidence of earlier oral/written warnings for a minor violation, when management believes the employee can still be rehabilitated.'],
 [7,'What checks precede dismissal in the handout?','Other disciplinary efforts have failed, and the committee confirms that the cause meets major-violation criteria in the policy manual.']
].map((x,i)=>({id:i,lesson:x[0],q:x[1],a:x[2]}));
